Fix: commit on a reserved range without reading /proc/self/maps every time

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0018-commit-without-proc-maps.patch
Status:     upstream performance bug (report to rexglue-sdk)

WHAT BROKE
  After a long session (a level played, the chest used many times, then
  back to the title), the title's own loading after the attract movie took
  23 s instead of 3-6 s. The game kept running but its time per frame CLIMBED
  through the load (13 -> 30 -> 48 -> 88 -> 153 ms): every frame cost more
  than the one before.

WHY
  rex::memory::AllocFixed (src/core/memory_posix.cpp) commits a range that is
  already reserved (mmap MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE fails with EEXIST) by mprotect,
  but first checked that the whole range is mapped by opening, reading and
  parsing ALL of /proc/self/maps (IsRangeFullyMapped). The game's loads commit
  physical memory over and over (MmAllocatePhysicalMemoryEx from the game's
  sub_82300E98). /proc/self/maps has one line per mapping, and a long session
  (and the load itself) splits the guest's reservations into ever more
  mappings, so each commit read a longer file.

FIX
  No pre-check: mprotect already fails with ENOMEM, changing nothing, when any
  page of the range is unmapped, which is exactly the case the check guarded
  against. IsRangeFullyMapped (now unused) is removed. (Protect and
  QueryProtect still read /proc/self/maps when asked for the old access; not
  seen in this game's hot paths.)

HOW FOUND
  The port's slow-load sampler (src/debug/slow_load_sampler.*: signals the
  main thread every 2 ms during a slow front-end load, logs where it was):
  90% of the samples under the game's sub_82300E98, 67% of them in
  std::basic_file::xsgetn (an ifstream read) and 3% in basic_file::open,
  called from rex::memory::AllocFixed; the reading loop is
  IsRangeFullyMapped.

--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -189,38 +189,6 @@
   return false;
 }
 
-// Check if [base, base+length) is fully covered by existing mappings (no gaps)
-static bool IsRangeFullyMapped(void* base_address, size_t length) {
-  if (!base_address || length == 0)
-    return false;
-
-  const uintptr_t begin = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(base_address);
-  const uintptr_t end = begin + length;
-  if (end < begin) {  // overflow check
-    return false;
-  }
-
-  std::ifstream maps("/proc/self/maps");
-  if (!maps.is_open())
-    return false;
-
-  uintptr_t cursor = begin;
-  std::string line;
-  while (std::getline(maps, line)) {
-    LinuxMapEntry e;
-    if (!ParseProcMapsLine(line, e))
-      continue;
-    if (e.end <= cursor)
-      continue;
-    if (e.start > cursor)
-      return false;  // gap found
-    cursor = e.end;
-    if (cursor >= end)
-      return true;
-  }
-  return cursor >= end;
-}
-
 // Convert /proc/self/maps permission chars to PageAccess
 static PageAccess PermsToPageAccess(const char perms[5]) {
   const bool r = perms[0] == 'r';
@@ -304,11 +272,15 @@
   if (errno == EEXIST && base_address &&
       (allocation_type == AllocationType::kCommit ||
        allocation_type == AllocationType::kReserveCommit)) {
-    // Verify the entire range is mapped before using mprotect
-    if (IsRangeFullyMapped(base_address, length)) {
-      if (mprotect(base_address, length, static_cast<int>(prot_requested)) == 0) {
-        return base_address;
-      }
+    // The range must be mapped all the way (a reservation) before mprotect
+    // changes it. mprotect itself checks that: it fails with ENOMEM when any
+    // page of the range isn't mapped, changing nothing. (It was checked first
+    // by reading and parsing all of /proc/self/maps, on every commit: that
+    // file grows with the process's mappings, so after a long session each
+    // commit took longer and a game's loading slowed to a crawl. Crash Mind
+    // over Mutant port, patch 0018.)
+    if (mprotect(base_address, length, static_cast<int>(prot_requested)) == 0) {
+      return base_address;
     }
   }
 #endif
